Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome
Respiratory System
Severe obesity, Excessive daytime sleepiness, Daytime hypercapnia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-031
Reason for Visit: New-onset morning confusion, severe dull morning headaches, and worsening leg swelling in a morbidly obese patient, requiring urgent assessment for hypercapnic respiratory failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a morbidly obese patient waking up confused, with a severe headache, and experiencing sudden worsening of lower limb edema. Urgent assessment was required to assess for acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure and right heart strain before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I woke up feeling like my head is going to explode, I can barely keep my eyes open, and my wife says I was making terrifying choking noises all night.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of excessive daytime sleepiness and chronic fatigue, alongside severe, disruptive snoring and witnessed apneas during sleep. Over the past 48 hours, they have experienced progressive exertional dyspnea and dull morning headaches that are significantly worse today. The spouse reports the patient was difficult to rouse this morning and appeared confused. The patient has noted rapidly increasing bilateral leg swelling over the last week.
Functional Status: Highly limited mobility secondary to severe obesity and extreme fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of sudden respiratory arrest during sleep; spouse is highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 22 breaths/min | SpO2: 89% on room air | Wt: 145 Kg
General: Severe obesity (BMI >40 kg/m2), plethoric appearance, drowsy and slow to respond during the interview, falling asleep between questions.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals distant breath sounds globally but no focal crackles or wheeze. Cardiovascular exam shows an elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP) and 3+ bilateral pitting peripheral edema to the knees. Abdominal exam reveals mild hepatomegaly. The clinical picture is highly suspicious for daytime hypercapnia secondary to hypoventilation, leading to secondary polycythemia and right-sided heart failure (cor pulmonale).
Environmental Observations: Patient is sleeping in a recliner chair; a broken/unused CPAP machine is visible in the corner.
Medication Reconciliation: On multiple antihypertensives; ceased CPAP use months ago due to mask discomfort.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical presentation, absence of focal neurological deficits (e.g., unilateral weakness, facial droop), and prominent respiratory/right heart signs.

Left-Sided Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lung fields without bibasilar crackles, and prominent snoring/apnea history pointing to a primary respiratory/ventilatory driver.

Simple Obstructive Sleep Apnea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of awake daytime hypoxemia, plethora, and overt right heart failure, indicating the ventilatory failure now extends into waking hours (OHS).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic triad of Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome (OHS): severe obesity, excessive daytime sleepiness, and signs of daytime hypercapnia/hypoxemia (morning headaches, plethora, right heart failure). The acute worsening of morning confusion, severe headache, and SpO2 of 89% on room air while awake indicates dangerous acute-on-chronic carbon dioxide retention. Delaying clinical assessment until the next in-hours period would carry a high risk of progressive hypercapnic coma or sudden cardiac arrhythmia. An urgent after-hours assessment was strictly mandated to assess the degree of ventilatory failure, administer careful controlled oxygen, and arrange immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for non-invasive ventilation (BiPAP) and arterial blood gas analysis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated continuous oxygen via nasal cannula carefully targeted to SpO2 88-92% to avoid abolishing the hypoxic drive. Activated EMS (000) for priority ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department for BiPAP initiation and diuresis.
Positive Behavioral Change: Weigh yourself immediately every morning after urinating; tracking this single metric provides objective proof of fluid retention, allowing you to seek medical help before the swelling crushes your heart's ability to pump.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable; hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the responding paramedics and receiving ED physician.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is being transferred via ambulance. The patient must be kept strictly awake and upright while awaiting transport to prevent further dangerous drops in oxygen and spikes in carbon dioxide.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The exact level of carbon dioxide in your blood must be measured with an arterial blood gas test at the hospital to guide your breathing machine settings.
Clinical Course: You require specialized breathing support (BiPAP) in the hospital to blow off the toxic carbon dioxide gas that is building up in your blood and causing your confusion and headaches.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not allow the patient to fall asleep while waiting for the ambulance; sleep immediately worsens the dangerous buildup of gas in the brain.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your weight is physically preventing your chest from taking deep enough breaths, causing toxic carbon dioxide to build up in your blood overnight; the hospital will use a specialized mask to push air in and flush this gas out.
Actionable Steps: You must wear the hospital's breathing mask exactly as instructed, even if it feels uncomfortable; removing the mask allows the toxic gas to rapidly build up again, directly leading to coma.

